1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
The record says: payment failed. What actually happened: the customer’s account was debited.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,300
Saturday 20 June, 6:48 pm. The bank behind Store 014’s tills is slow to answer UPI payments.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:15,700
The till waits 30 seconds, hears nothing, and shows “failed: no response”. The cashier asks for another way to pay.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:21,600
Payments operations sees approvals fall to 69% and 67% for two hours, then recover. Incident closed.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:28,000
Reconciliation finds 17 UPI credits with no approved payment at the till, and parks them in suspense.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,900
Customer support takes 26 payment complaints in June. 23 say “I was charged twice”.

7
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:39,800
Finance sees no UPI fees in June and ₹11,302 of refunds. Nothing looks wrong.

8
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:46,700
What actually happened: her bank had taken the money. The credit came 87 seconds later. She paid twice, and waited 8 days.

9
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:53,100
Payment success in those 90 minutes: 61% at the till, 82% at the bank, 100% of bills paid. 87% charged once.

10
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:59,500
Put the bill ID on every UPI request: each double charge is found the next morning. Median refund: 4.9 days → 0.7.

11
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,500
Every record was right about what it saw. Only the customer saw both debits.
